# <app> auto-deploy -- oneshot service that runs deploy-pull.sh once.
#
# Copy to `deploy/<app>-deploy.service` in your app repo, adapt <app>/<user>/paths, and install
# on the box as a SYSTEM unit under /etc/systemd/system/. Install steps are under "Installing the
# pull timer" in the template README -- relative paths won't resolve from your repo:
#   https://github.com/Rackbops/rackbops-web-deploy-template/blob/main/servers/nginx-static/README.md
#
# It runs as a system unit but drops to <user> via User= below -- that drop-to-user form is exactly
# why it's a system unit, not a `--user` one. Dropping to a DIFFERENT user is the part an ordinary
# user manager can't do: systemd.exec says that for "user services of any other user" than root,
# "switching user identity is not permitted, hence the only valid setting is the same user the
# user's service manager is running as". So User= is accepted in a user unit, it just can't name
# anyone else there. Paired with the .timer, which schedules it. The service body stays
# privilege-light: no docker, no sudo -- it only pulls and logs (installing the unit needs sudo;
# running it does not).
#
# No Environment=HOME= line: systemd.exec's SetLoginEnvironment= "defaults to true if User= ...
# [is] set", so $HOME is set automatically from User= above -- an explicit line here is redundant.
#
# TimeoutStartSec= matters because Type=oneshot disables systemd's start timeout by DEFAULT (unlike
# every other service type, per systemd.service(5)) -- without it, a hung `git pull` (a
# stalled-but-open network session with no keepalive to catch it -- SSH or HTTPS, the box clones
# over either) can park this unit running forever, and since the paired .timer's OnUnitActiveSec=
# only re-arms from the last *activation*, an already-running unit just... keeps running, with no
# visible error. On timeout systemd marks the unit failed and kills its cgroup, letting the timer's
# next cycle try again -- sufficient on its own, though a consumer can also add a faster-triggering
# complementary guard: ConnectTimeout=/ServerAliveInterval=/ServerAliveCountMax= to whatever SSH
# alias deploy-pull.sh's git pull uses over SSH, or http.lowSpeedLimit/http.lowSpeedTime for an
# HTTPS clone (see the template README's pull-model section for both).
#
# The 4min default has no basis beyond suiting a small hand-authored one-pager (the reference
# consumer) -- raise it for a bigger repo or a slow uplink. Git's smart-protocol fetch has no
# resume: a pull that legitimately needs longer than the cap gets killed, retried next tick, and
# killed again -- every attempt re-downloads the pack from zero, so it never completes. That is a
# livelock, not a slow deploy, and a single failed run looks identical in the journal to the
# one-off hang this setting exists to catch.
#
# Raising it does not need to stay under the paired .timer's OnUnitActiveSec=. Per
# systemd.timer(5), OnUnitActiveSec= is relative to when the unit it fires was last ACTIVATED --
# not to each tick, and not to when a run finishes -- so a tick landing mid-run is simply skipped,
# never queued alongside the one already running. Once that run ends (success, or this timeout's
# own kill), the schedule is already overdue and catches up on its own within the existing poll
# cadence; a longer timeout only delays how soon a genuine hang gets caught, it doesn't add a
# second, separate stall.
#
# WHAT THE KILL LEAVES BEHIND depends on which phase it lands in. Killing the FETCH is clean --
# nothing is left over and the next run simply refetches. Killing the CHECKOUT is not: git writes
# worktree files one at a time, so an interrupted checkout can leave TRACKED files that no longer
# match HEAD, or -- if the incoming commit was ADDING a file -- an UNTRACKED one sitting at a path
# git now wants to create, which blocks a pull just as surely. A SIGKILL can also leave
# .git/index.lock behind. Nothing here clears itself, and the web root is served straight off that
# worktree, so the half-applied tree stays live.
#
# Two shapes, and the quieter one is the dangerous one. If the incoming commit touches the damaged
# path, the next `git pull --ff-only` REFUSES ("Your local changes ... would be overwritten") --
# loud, and the unit fails every tick. If it does not touch that path, the pull SUCCEEDS and
# leaves the damage alone, because a fast-forward only writes paths that differ between the two
# commits: without help that is exit 0, unit green, and a broken file served indefinitely.
# deploy-pull.sh therefore also compares the worktree against HEAD after a successful pull, and
# logs a NOTE when they differ -- a note rather than a failure, because a submodule bump, a
# filemode change or an app writing to a tracked path all look identical here, and failing the
# unit on those would break a working deployment.
#
# It reports and never repairs: a dirty tree is indistinguishable from someone having edited files
# on the box. Recovery is `git reset --hard @{u}` -- it overwrites both a mismatched tracked file
# and an untracked one colliding with an incoming path, discards a box-side commit, and leaves
# every OTHER untracked file alone. `@{u}` is the part that matters: plain `reset --hard` with no
# ref targets current HEAD, which is a no-op here. A submodule bump needs `git submodule update`
# instead.

[Unit]
Description=<app> -- pull the latest site from GitHub
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
TimeoutStartSec=4min
User=<user>
ExecStart=/bin/bash /opt/stacks/<app>/deploy/deploy-pull.sh
